f 17592186548492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548901 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "46647a5c-2b95-53fe-940a-e6b35e9ddd8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548928 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "829f2828-1700-5400-9439-0d028651d780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y+3=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548981 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+1}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{2}) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/96e1647f6069dee4219f8cb92e3fb4277396394c016b27ecd4de557f87257000.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e648a6d-37fa-5da0-b09f-42f9d00baf66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549003 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(3,-2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-6),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(-1)t,\\,y+2=(-1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "727c8803-de94-5b7f-a91c-cc70ef9900c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549012 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =2(x-4),\\,t=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=2(x-4)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(2,3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ba3cd0d-7f42-50d0-93f6-108a716173e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549026 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,3),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5=2pt \\\\ 5=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =p{(\\frac{5}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{25}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=2(\\frac{5}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
